{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And you shall not eat any blood, in all your dwellings, whether of bird or beast.</b> This excludes the blood of fish and locusts, and blood of eggs. And it is known that the prohibition of blood of eggs is only rabbinic, and specifically when it is found in the yolk; but if found in the white, one casts away the blood and eats the rest — this is the opinion of the Rav Alfasi, of blessed memory." "<b>But</b> the opinion of the Ba'alei ha-Tosafot, of blessed memory, is that when the blood is found at the knot — whether at the knot of the yolk or at the knot of the white — it is forbidden. And the reason it is forbidden rabbinically when the blood is found in the yolk, where all agree it is forbidden, is because of the concern of place (חשש מקום): for one must suspect that from that place where the blood is found, from there the egg began to take shape, and it turns out that one who eats this egg transgresses on account of a crawling thing that crawls upon the earth."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:23:46.737981708Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}